    {
      "id": "q5-rpg",
      "track": "section-b",
      "title": "Question 5 · Regular Polygon Graph",
      "markdown": "A Regular Polygon Graph (RPG in short) is a graph consisting of a sequence of non-overlapping regular polygons of 3 - 6 sides. The centres of the polygons lie on the same horizontal line. Each polygon shares an edge and two vertices with the polygon on the left of it (if it is not the leftmost polygon) and shares an edge and two vertices with the polygon on the right of it (if it is not the rightmost polygon). For example, the figure below shows a RPG.\n\nThe RPG above has 12 vertices and 15 edges.\n\nDefine the diagonal distance of a RPG to be the length of the shortest path connecting P and Q, where P is the leftmost vertex among the topmost vertices, and Q is the rightmost vertex among the bottommost vertices. For example, the RPG above has diagonal distance equals to 3.\n\nA RPG can be represented by a sequence of numbers, which specifies the number of sides of the polygons from left to right. For example, the RPG above is represented by 5643."
    }
  ],
